-- title:
--   Branch primes and power-series readings of a crossing presentation
-- statement:
--   Let $W$ be a commutative domain which is a discrete valuation ring, complete for the adic topology of its maximal ideal, let $\pi$ be an element with $\mathfrak m_W=(\pi)$, and let $E\ge 1$. Let $O$ be a noetherian local commutative ring and let $\iota$ be a ring isomorphism from the adic completion of $O$ at its maximal ideal onto $\mathrm{UVCrossingModel}\,W\,(\pi^E)=\mathrm{MvPowerSeries}(\mathrm{Fin}\,2,W)/(X_0X_1-C(\pi^E))$. Assume given $t\in O$ whose image in the completion satisfies $\iota(t)=\mathrm{const}\,(\pi^E)\,\pi$, the class of the constant series $C(\pi)$; elements $c_x,c_y\in O$ and units $\gamma_U,\gamma_V$ of the crossing model with $\iota(c_x)=\gamma_U\cdot U(\pi^E)$ and $\iota(c_y)=\gamma_V\cdot V(\pi^E)$; and prime ideals $P_V,P_U$ of $O$ with $t\in P_V$, $c_y\in P_V$, $c_x\notin P_V$, $t\in P_U$, $c_x\in P_U$, $c_y\notin P_U$. The conclusion has four parts. First, for all $f\in O$: $f\in P_V$ iff $\iota(f)$ lies in the ideal generated by $\mathrm{const}\,(\pi^E)\,\pi$ and $V(\pi^E)$; and $f\in P_U$ iff $\iota(f)$ lies in the ideal generated by $\mathrm{const}\,(\pi^E)\,\pi$ and $U(\pi^E)$. Next, there is a ring homomorphism $\varphi_V\colon O\to \kappa[[X]]$, $\kappa$ the residue field of $W$, such that $\varphi_V(f)=0$ exactly for $f\in P_V$, $\varphi_V$ carries the maximal ideal of $O$ into the series with vanishing constant coefficient, $\varphi_V(c_x)=uX$ for some unit $u$ of $\kappa[[X]]$, and whenever $\varphi_V(f)\neq 0$ has order $n$ there is a unit $\gamma$ of the crossing model with $\iota(f)-\gamma\cdot U(\pi^E)^n$ in the ideal generated by $\mathrm{const}\,(\pi^E)\,\pi$ and $V(\pi^E)$. Finally, the same statement with the roles of $U$ and $V$, of $c_x$ and $c_y$, and of $P_V$ and $P_U$ interchanged, giving $\varphi_U\colon O\to\kappa[[X]]$.
--
--   This is the dictionary between the two branch primes through a split ordinary double point of thickness $E$ and a crossing presentation of the completed local ring: the branch primes are recognised as the contractions of the formal branches $(\pi,V)$ and $(\pi,U)$, and reduction modulo one branch reads $O$ inside $\kappa[[X]]$, with the order of the reading controlling the power of the complementary coordinate appearing modulo that branch. It is used in the analysis of crossing charts on modular curves, where the branch readings are compared with level automorphisms and blow-up charts.
-- source:
